In this model, angfrus and calm are parallel mediators.

one_ind <- 'disob_f ~ home_f + angfrus + calm # c and b paths

            angfrus ~ home_f
            calm ~ home_f # a path
            calm~~angfrus'

They are two different paths:

home_f -> angfrus -> disob_f
home_f -> calm -> disob_f

This will work:

out_fit1_angfrus <- indirect_effect(x = "home_f",
                                    m = c("angfrus"),
                                    y = "disob_f",
                                    fit = fit1,
                                    boot_ci = TRUE,
                                    R = 10,
                                    seed = 2345)
out_fit1_clam <- indirect_effect(x = "home_f",
                                 m = c("calm"),
                                 y = "disob_f",
                                 fit = fit1,
                                 boot_ci = TRUE,
                                 R = 10,
                                 seed = 2345)

Make sure that you set the seed, to ensure that the same B bootstrap samples are used across analyses, or use do_boot() to generate bootstrap estimates that can be reused across analyses. (But see the next paragraph on bootstrapping with clustering ...)

By the way, I noticed that you used cluster. I am not sure how lavaan handles bootstrapping when 'cluster' is used. Bootstrapping conducted in manymome tries to mimic what lavaan does, to ensure the results are consistent with those of lavaan. If done internally, manymome uses casewise resampling, which ignores clustering. I am not sure how lavaan does bootstrapping when the argument 'cluster' is used. (I would like to know more about this but will ask in another post.)

If you would like to form CIs for the indirect effects while using MLR with clustering in sem(), you can use the Monte Carlo confidence intervals. It uses the variance-covariance matrix generated by lavaan to form the Monte Carlo CI intervals, and so you can use whatever estimation method you like when calling sem(). E.g.,

out_fit1_angfrus <- indirect_effect(x = "home_f",
                                    m = c("angfrus"),
                                    y = "disob_f",
                                    fit = fit1,
                                    mc_ci = TRUE,
                                    R = 10,
                                    seed = 4567)

Again, the two mediators are in parallel and you need two calls to indirect_effect(). You should set the seed to the same number, or pregenerage the MC simulated estimates using do_mc() and reuse the output (https://sfcheung.github.io/manymome/articles/do_mc.html).

Hope this helps.

-- Shu Fai

